## What to write

Every item below is an instruction about the page, never text to copy into it. Start the page with the line `# {{ ctx.repo_name }}: overview`.

1. Opening, under that title: 2 or 3 plain sentences saying what the project is (the kind of software, in the README's own terms but not its tagline or slogan) and who uses it for what. Then one short scope sentence naming the onboarding pages for setup and for how it works. No praise adjectives.
2. `## Using it`: the 3 to 5 things a user actually does with it, as a numbered list. Each item is one line: the command or call in backticks exactly as the material shows it, then what it gives the user, then the citation of the span that shows it (a command's own declaration, or the README or guide section that shows the step). Only things the README, user documentation or the lists above show.
3. `## How the parts fit`: 2 to 4 short paragraphs telling the path a command or request takes through the parts, in order: who starts it, which part receives it, what each part hands to the next, and what is stored or returned. Use the import list for direction and the part descriptions for roles; refer to a part by its page title in bold. End a paragraph with a citation only when a README, guide or declaration span above supports it; a part has no span of its own, so never make one up.
4. {% if m and m.journeys %}`## What happens when it runs`: a list with one item per walkthrough page above, each ONE sentence of at most 30 words saying what starts it and what it leaves behind, then "Walkthrough: **<page title>**". The How It Works page tells each journey in full, so give no stages, branches or early exits here.{% else %}No section on what happens when it runs: no walkthrough pages are written yet.{% endif %}
5. `## The parts`: a table `Part | What it owns | Open it when`, one row per top-level part above (at most 8, in the order given), the part's page title in bold. "What it owns" is what the part's code is responsible for, taken from that part's own line above and written about the code ("Parses source files and stores the index"), never about what users do with it ("Users use this code to ...", "lets users ..."). At most 15 words per cell. No citations in the table and no prose after it.
6. {% if m and m.terms %}`## Words you will meet`: at most 6 terms from the defined-terms list that a reader meets on this page or the next ones, one sentence each in plain words, each ending with its source path in backticks.{% else %}No terms section: the documentation defines no terms.{% endif %}
7. `## New here? Read in this order`: a numbered list of 3 to 5 pages from "Pages a reader can open next", each its title in bold and half a line on why, with no citation.

Rules:
- Write only these sections. No paragraphs before or after a list, table or diagram.
- Do not report counts of files, imports, hotspots, cycles or clusters.
- Never call a part local, hosted or remote unless the material says where it runs, and never give a part the project's own name.
- Repository files are cited only as backticked repository-relative paths, never as Markdown links or absolute paths.
{% if not (style_states_length | default(false)) %}- Length: about 700 to 900 words.
{% endif %}
